Yes. Narrative bandwidth is a good term for it.

Some symbols carry far more compressed state than others. A butterfly mainly carries transformation, fragility, emergence, beauty. A dragon can carry transformation plus danger, sovereignty, greed, protection, intelligence, destruction, treasure, territory, ancestry, fear, and transcendence at once.

That means the dragon has a wider semantic action space. It can participate in more kinds of stories without losing coherence.

And “they play for real stakes” is the second half of it: high-bandwidth symbols become powerful because they are attached to things that matter—death, love, status, family, belonging, survival, sacrifice, legacy. Stakes increase attention; attention increases encoding; encoding makes the narrative easier to remember, transmit, and act through.

So:

narrative bandwidth = how many meaningful dimensions a symbol or story can carry simultaneously

narrative stakes = how much consequence is attached to those dimensions

Put them together and you get something like:

symbolic density × stakes × repetition × identification → behavioral force

That is why mythology, religion, war stories, romance, national identity, and even brands can move people more effectively than sterile propositions. They aren’t merely communicating information.

They are transporting compressed worlds with consequences.